A||N||5||gtcash||V8 which compressor?||||1||
Z||000000||gtcash||06-13-2001||12:44 AM||||What compressor did you use in your conversion? Sandon or Fiero? Also, what's the procedure to remove that #1 sparkplug?<BR>Drop the Alt?, Drop Alt & Comp? As you can tell, I'm about to install the Alt & Comp, and would like to be able to change plugs at a latter date.  Help!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Chris McDonald||06-13-2001||11:44 AM||||Go with the a/c Compressor off of an LT-1 Equiped Camaro or Firebird.  If you are using the V Belt pulley, you will have to get a regular Fiero clutch and pulley and swap it with the F-Body compressor.  It is already 134 compatable and makes for a very nice piece.  You will have to make the same modifications to the housing as a regular Fiero compressor to fit the V8 application (assuming you are using an Archie style kit).<P>Get your local a/c shop (sometimes NAPA) to make up a custom hose set to connect from the compressor to the original body hard lines.  Napa has a nice little harness conversion that will plug into the Fiero harness and directly into the newer style compressor.<P>As far as the #1 plug goes, it is best accessed with the Alternator out of the way, or with it loose on the belt so you have room to work.  <P>I highly suggest you make a braket to go from the back of the compressor to the block.  This will help keep the belt tight and reduce other problems.  You can make one out of a variety of other brackets. <P>I know for sure that the older Archie kits did not include such a bracket and you can have trouble keeping firm tension on the belt as the compressor kicks in and out.  He may offer a bracket now.  However, if you have a welder (or a friend that welds), grinder, imagination, and access to plenty of stock rear brackets I am sure you can make one.  You will want to bolt it to the original V8 motor mount holes and then branch out.  <P>I made mine hold both the compressor and matching adjustable bracket for the back of the alternator.<P>Have fun.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtcash||06-14-2001||12:14 AM||||Why the Camero compressor? Is it physicaly smaller? Or what?  And why the Fiero clutch and pully? Are you familiar with the Sandon compressors? Why didn't you use one?<BR>Man, all I can say is Why, Why, Why!!!<BR>Thanks for telling me how to change a plug! I guess it's the price you pay to have a V8.<BR>And a bracket to the rear of the compressor and altenator, good idea, I'll do it!<BR>How about an idler pulley? Archie shows one for a better wrap on the compressor.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000003||Chris McDonald||06-14-2001||01:36 PM||||Why the F-body compressor?-Simple.  It is physically nearly the same size as the Fiero, is better built, plentiful at the local salvage yard (cheap), and has the same front bolting pattern as the Fiero.  If doing the Archie kit you have to cut the compressor to clear.  You will cut the F-Body compressor in the exact same way.  <P>Yes, I am know the other compressor you mention very well.  I am sure you can make it work.  However, it uses two individual screw on lines that will be difficult to clear in the Fiero application.  The F-Body and Fiero compressor dump straight out the rear with a one piece two line manifold.  <P>The other compressor is fairly expensive.  <P>Finally, why the Fiero Clutch?  Simple, you will most likely be using a standard V Belt on the crank and alternator.  This is the same as the Fiero V-belt.  There is a simple tool you can buy at any A/C jobber shop that will allow you to pull the clutch and swap them very easy.  <P>If you are running a serpentine pulley on the other accesories, then maybe you can run the original F-body clutch and pulley.  <P>Oh--the idler pulley.  If you can figure out how to install one that actually helps the system work better--then go for it.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||gtcash||06-14-2001||11:36 PM||||They dump straight out the rear... must get very close to the exhaust header??? I do have a Archie kit, but it is very old, 1988...I'm re-doing the engine, and all the stuff it takes to make it a CLEAN install. I appreciate your replys, is yours carburated?<BR>If so, what air cleaner, and did you have to put a scoop on the deck lid to clear it?<BR>Mine, they just cut a hole in the lid, and when you park you put a pot over the carb to keep the rain and hail, snow, nesting birds, and whatever out!<BR>Glenn <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Chris McDonald||06-15-2001||02:59 PM||||I hope you are joking about the deck lid hole.  <P>I built mine in late 98-99 and sold it last year.  I can still live vicariously through guys like you.<P>Yes, they dump out right next to the exhaust pipe where it meets the manifold.  <P>No hood scoop needed if you use the edelbrock foam air cleaner like you see on almost all the Archie cars.<P>Get a new deck lid!||||reg||1
